<Page title="About" heading="Screen videos should behave like you meant them to." eyebrow="About" description="Why MeCorder exists, and the principles it's built on.">
  <p>Most screen recorders capture pixels. Then the real work starts: finding every click, placing every zoom, timing every highlight, and doing it all again when the recording changes.</p>
  <p>MeCorder starts from a different place. It records what happened, not just what it looked like: which element you clicked, what you typed into, where you scrolled. That makes it possible to describe a video by its <strong>intent</strong>, as a Program, and let the editor do the frame-by-frame work.</p>
  <h2 id="principles">Principles</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Intent first, Timeline always.</strong> Programs write ordinary keyframes and effects. You can always open the Timeline and adjust any of them by hand.</li>
    <li><strong>Never guess.</strong> If a click has no name, a Program skips it and says so, rather than aiming at the wrong thing.</li>
    <li><strong>Your Mac, your recordings.</strong> Recording, editing, captions and export happen on your Mac. Nothing is uploaded.</li>
    <li><strong>One undo step.</strong> Whatever a Program, the Ask box or the AI Director does, ⌘Z takes it back in one go.</li>
    <li><strong>A native Mac app.</strong> Built with Apple’s frameworks, designed to feel at home next to the apps you already use.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2 id="status">Where things are</h2>
  <p>MeCorder is in active development and hasn’t had a public release yet. The <a href={u('/changelog')}>changelog</a> follows the work as it lands, and the <a href={u('/docs')}>documentation</a> describes what’s built today.</p>
</Page>
